# Template CODEOWNERS for a repo adopting this workflow.
#
# Copy this file to `.github/CODEOWNERS` (or `CODEOWNERS` at repo root — GitHub accepts either)
# in your own repo, then replace the placeholder handles below with real GitHub usernames or
# teams. CODEOWNERS only has teeth once branch protection requires review from the matching
# owner on affected files — see conventions/branch-protection.md in this repo for that setup.
#
# GitHub evaluates rules top-to-bottom and the LAST matching rule wins, so put your most general
# rule first and more specific overrides below it — the opposite order of a firewall/gitignore.

# Default: every file needs review from the core maintainer group.
*                               @your-org/maintainers

# The workflow's own gates and CI wiring get pinned reviewers — the exact set of files this
# design's Gate-1 approval named as needing extra scrutiny, since they're what makes the other
# two gates trustworthy: nobody can quietly loosen the ship-gate hook or the CI checks that
# enforce it in the same PR that also ships product code.
/.github/                      @your-org/maintainers
/hooks/ship-gate.sh            @your-org/maintainers

# Design artifacts are a governance surface in their own right (they're what Gate 1 approves) —
# route them to whoever owns the design-review process, which may be a narrower group than
# "everyone who can review code".
/design-artifacts/             @your-org/design-reviewers
